---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedPlaybooks from '../../components/playbooks/RelatedPlaybooks.astro';

const today = '2026-07-04T00:00:00+00:00';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'What is the Best Answer Hub SMB AI Readiness Score?', a: 'The Best Answer Hub SMB AI Readiness Score is a free, 15-minute self-assessment for small and medium businesses. It asks 30 questions across five pillars, then returns a 0 to 100 score and one of four readiness bands. It runs entirely in your browser with no signup, no email, and no vendor agenda.' },
  { q: 'What does "AI readiness" mean for a small business?', a: 'AI readiness is whether your business can use AI usefully without wasting money. It depends on five things: organized data, connected tools, written-down processes, team skills to check AI output, and a simple governance rule. Most small firms have a workflow gap, not a technology gap.' },
  { q: 'Is my business too small for AI?', a: 'No. Readiness is about fit, not headcount. A tidy solo business can be readier than a disorganized 40-person firm. The smallest firms most often assume AI does not apply to them, and that assumption is usually the thing holding them back, not their size.' },
  { q: 'How do I know if my business is ready for AI?', a: 'Score yourself on the five pillars: data, tools, process, skills, and governance. If most are reasonable and one is weak, you are close and should fix that gap. The free <a href="/knowledge-center/smb-ai-readiness/">Best Answer Hub SMB AI Readiness Score</a> measures all five in about 15 minutes and flags your weakest two.' },
  { q: 'What are the five pillars of AI readiness?', a: 'The five pillars are data (is it organized and findable), tools and tech (do systems connect), process (is the work repeatable and documented), skills (can the team check AI output), and governance (is there a rule for what is allowed). A business is only as ready as its weakest pillar.' },
  { q: 'What are the four AI readiness levels?', a: 'The four bands are AI-Curious (0 to 40, exploring), AI-Aware (45 to 65, ad-hoc use), AI-Ready (70 to 85, using AI with intent), and AI-Mature (90 to 100, AI embedded). Each band points to one next move rather than a grade.' },
  { q: 'What is the difference between AI readiness and AI maturity?', a: 'AI readiness is whether a business can start using AI safely and usefully today. AI maturity is how deeply AI is already built into the business. Readiness is the on-ramp; maturity is the destination. The four bands run from the readiness end (AI-Curious) to the maturity end (AI-Mature).' },
  { q: 'What should a small business do first with AI?', a: 'Start with one painful, repeatable weekly task, not a tool. Test a single AI tool against it for 30 days and measure the result. If it does not clearly help, drop it and try the next task. This keeps spend small and tied to a real outcome.' },
  { q: 'Do I need clean, organized data before I can use AI?', a: 'You do not need perfect data, but you do need findable data for the task at hand. Rather than a big cleanup project, organize only the data the first use case needs. Poor data quality is one of the most common reasons AI projects stall, so anchor any tidying to a specific use.' },
  { q: 'Can I use AI without a technical team?', a: 'Yes. Most small businesses adopt AI through ready-made tools that need no coding. The skill that matters is judgment: writing a clear prompt, checking the output, and knowing when it is wrong. That is a team habit, not a technical role, and it is one of the five readiness pillars.' },
  { q: 'What are the signs my business is not ready for AI?', a: 'Warning signs include not being able to name a time-wasting weekly task, key data living in inboxes or someone’s head, staff already using AI tools with no rules, and wanting AI to fix a process you have never documented. These point to fixing foundations first, not buying a tool.' },
  { q: 'Why do small business AI projects fail?', a: 'They mostly fail for business reasons, not technical ones. Gartner attributes abandoned generative AI projects to poor data quality, weak controls, rising costs, and unclear business value. For a small firm, that is wasted capacity it cannot spare, which is why diagnosing readiness before buying matters.' },
  { q: 'Does my small business need an AI policy?', a: 'If anyone on your team uses AI tools at work, a basic one-page policy is worth having. It states what data can be entered, which tools are approved, and who signs off on AI output. It is the simplest of the five pillars to fix and closes a common, quiet risk.' },
  { q: 'How long does it take to get AI-ready?', a: 'For most small businesses, closing a single readiness gap takes a few weeks of focused effort, not months. Readiness improves one pillar at a time. The point is not to fix everything at once, but to close the one gap that is currently blocking a useful first use case.' },
  { q: 'How is this different from a vendor’s free AI quiz?', a: 'Most free AI quizzes are built to recommend the maker’s own product, and many gate the result behind your contact details for sales follow-up. The Best Answer Hub SMB AI Readiness Score sells no tools, carries no affiliate links, captures no email, and is free to tell you to wait. The diagnosis is the deliverable, not a lead.' },
];

const seo = {
  title: 'Is Your Small Business Ready for AI? A 2026 Readiness Guide',
  description: 'What AI-ready means for a small business, the five pillars that decide it, the four readiness levels, and how to find your level free in about 15 minutes.',
  url: '/playbooks/small-business-ai-readiness/',
  type: 'WebPage' as const,
  datePublished: today,
  dateModified: today,
  breadcrumbs: [
    { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
    { name: 'Playbooks', url: '/playbooks/' },
    { name: 'Is Your Small Business Ready for AI?', url: '/playbooks/small-business-ai-readiness/' },
  ],
  faq: faqs.map(f => ({ question: f.q, answer: f.a.replace(/<[^>]*>/g, '').replace(/\s{2,}/g, ' ').trim() })),
};
---

<BaseLayout seo={seo}>
